Take a reference on the reqid quick mode reuses, instead of just reading it.

This is the fault 0004 was added to find, and the instrumentation caught it on
the first try. An IKEv1 CHILD_SA rekey nets *zero* references on the reqid the
new SA inherits:

    20:11:57 reqid 1: referenced       (refs 2, remote 10.100.1.220/32)
    20:11:57 CHILD_SA ITM-Office-1{7} CREATED => INSTALLING
    20:11:58 reqid 1: reused by reqid  (refs 3, remote 10.100.1.220/32)
    20:11:58 reqid 1: released         (refs 2, remote 10.100.1.220/32)
    20:11:58 CHILD_SA ITM-Office-1{7} INSTALLING => INSTALLED
    20:11:58 reqid 1: released         (refs 1, remote 10.100.1.220/32)  <- extra

1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1. The new child ends up holding the reqid with no reference
of its own, so when the gateway deletes the *old* child ten minutes later the
entry drops to zero, is destroyed, and its number goes onto the recycle pool
while the new child is still installed and using it:

    20:21:48 reqid 1: released to zero, recycling (refs 0, remote 10.100.1.220/32)

`alloc_reqid` then hands that number to the next child that cannot inherit its
own, and two CHILD_SAs with different traffic selectors share one reqid:

    20:59:31 reqid 3: ref found no entry
    20:59:31 reqid 1: RECYCLED from the released pool (refs 1, remote 10.238.96.0/24)

From there `ipsec_processor.c` resolves an outbound SA with
`checkout_by_reqid(policy->get_reqid(policy))` -- `find_first` over a list built
with `insert_first` -- so both selectors get whichever SA was installed last and
the loser's packets are dropped by the gateway on a selector mismatch. Its
inbound direction keeps working, which is what makes the fault look impossible
from the client. Measured: 10.100.1.220 dead while 10.238.96.94 and
192.168.1.56 answered, both children on reqid 1.

The extra release is the quick mode task's own destroy():

    if (this->child.reqid)
    {
        charon->kernel->release_reqid(charon->kernel, this->child.reqid);
    }

`check_for_rekeyed_child()` fills that field with `get_reqid()`, a plain getter
that takes no reference, so the release is unmatched. That it is an oversight
rather than intent is settled by `use_reqid()` a few lines below, which sets the
same field and *does* ref first, releasing the previous value only once the new
one is held.

`get_reqid_ref()` exists for exactly this and returns 0 when it cannot take the
reference -- leaving child.reqid at 0, so destroy() then correctly releases
nothing and the new SA falls back to allocating by traffic selector.

Upstream bug, present in 5.9.14; IKEv1 only, since quick mode is the IKEv1
rekey path.

--- orig/src/libcharon/sa/ikev1/tasks/quick_mode.c	2026-09-16 21:04:57.419194900 +0200
+++ work/src/libcharon/sa/ikev1/tasks/quick_mode.c	2026-09-16 21:04:57.598604600 +0200
@@ -1033,7 +1033,12 @@
